dict(sec=1, img='wiggers', cap='Normal cardiac cycle diagram with four valve events numbered',
stem="A 29-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 4-month history of shortness of breath when she climbs stairs. She emigrated from rural Nepal 2 years ago and recalls months of painful, swollen joints as a child. Her pulse is 92/min and regular, respirations are 16/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Cardiac examination shows a sharp, high-pitched sound shortly after the second heart sound, followed by a low-pitched rumble at the apex that is heard best with the bell while she lies on her left side. No murmur is heard during systole.{FIG}The sharp sound in this patient occurs at which of the following numbered points on the cardiac cycle diagram?",
choices=["Between points 3 and 4","Point 1","Point 2","Point 3","Point 4"], key=4,
exp="The sharp early-diastolic sound followed by an apical rumble is the <b>opening snap of mitral stenosis</b>; childhood rheumatic fever in a patient from a region where it is common is the usual cause. A normal valve opens silently. A stiff, fused mitral valve snaps open at the instant <b>left ventricular pressure falls below left atrial pressure</b>, which is the end of isovolumetric relaxation (point 4).<br>• Point 1: ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure → mitral closure → first heart sound (S1).<br>• Point 2: ventricular pressure rises above aortic pressure (about 80 mm Hg) → aortic opening.<br>• Point 3: ventricular pressure falls below aortic pressure → aortic closure → second heart sound (S2) and the dicrotic notch.<br>• Point 4: ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure → mitral opening → opening snap.<br>The higher the left atrial pressure, the sooner the valve is forced open, so a shorter S2-to-snap interval means more severe stenosis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> An opening snap is heard when the mitral valve opens, the moment left ventricular pressure falls below left atrial pressure at the end of isovolumetric relaxation.",
wrong={0:"Between points 3 and 4 is isovolumetric relaxation: all four valves are closed, so no valve can open or snap. This gap is the S2-to-opening-snap interval itself.",
1:"Point 1 is mitral and tricuspid closure, the first heart sound at the start of systole. The snap in this patient follows the second heart sound.",
2:"Point 2 is aortic valve opening. A stiff aortic or pulmonic valve makes an ejection click here, just after S1, not after S2.",
3:"Point 3 is aortic valve closure (A2 of the second heart sound and the dicrotic notch). The snap comes after S2, not with it."},
eli5="Healthy valves open without a sound. A stiff, scarred mitral valve pops open with a snap the instant the ventricle's pressure drops below the atrium's, just after the second heart sound."),

