dict(sec=1, img=None,
stem="An investigator studies isolated muscle strips from the same animal. When the resting length of a papillary muscle preparation is increased by 10%, the active tension it develops on stimulation rises. When the resting length of a gastrocnemius preparation is increased by the same proportion, its active tension falls.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the difference between the two preparations?",
choices=["Cardiac muscle rests below the length for optimal overlap","Cardiac thick filaments lengthen as the sarcomere is stretched","Skeletal muscle cannot add motor units in an isolated strip","Stretch opens L-type calcium channels only in cardiac muscle","Stretch releases titin's tension only in skeletal muscle"], key=0,
exp="Force depends on how many cross-bridges can form, which depends on filament overlap. <b>Skeletal muscle rests near the optimal length</b>, the peak of its length–tension curve, so further stretch pulls the filaments apart and lowers force. <b>Cardiac muscle works on the ascending limb</b>: at normal filling its sarcomeres are shorter than optimal, so more stretch → better overlap (and greater calcium sensitivity of troponin C) → more force. In the whole heart this is <b>Starling's law</b>: more filling, more stroke volume.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Cardiac muscle operates on the ascending limb of the length–tension curve, which is why greater preload increases force (the Frank–Starling mechanism); skeletal muscle rests near its optimum.",
wrong={1:"Filaments do not change length with stretch; only their overlap changes.",
2:"Motor-unit recruitment is irrelevant in an isolated, directly stimulated strip, and it would not explain why the cardiac strip gains force with stretch.",
3:"Stretch does not selectively open L-type channels. Length-dependent activation in cardiac muscle comes mainly from filament overlap and troponin C's calcium sensitivity.",
4:"Titin's passive tension rises with stretch in both muscles; it does not explain the opposite responses."},
eli5="A heart muscle cell at rest is a little bunched up, so stretching it lines the fibers up better and it pulls harder. A leg muscle is already lined up perfectly, so stretching it only pulls the fibers apart."),

dict(sec=1, img=None,
stem="A healthy 24-year-old man performs a Valsalva maneuver during an examination. When he releases the strain, venous return to the right ventricle surges, and within two to three beats left ventricular stroke volume rises to match the larger right ventricular output. The same beat-to-beat matching of the two ventricles is seen in recipients of a heart transplant, whose hearts have no autonomic innervation.{FIG}Which of the following mechanisms is chiefly responsible for matching left ventricular output to right ventricular output?",
choices=["Atrial natriuretic peptide release","Bainbridge reflex","Baroreceptor reflex","Frank–Starling mechanism","Sympathetic increase in contractility"], key=3,
exp="The extra blood the right ventricle pumps reaches the left atrium and raises <b>left ventricular preload</b>. More stretch → better filament overlap → greater force → larger left ventricular stroke volume. This is the <b>Frank–Starling mechanism</b>, intrinsic to the muscle, so it works without nerves or hormones — which is why it still matches the two ventricles in a denervated transplanted heart. At any steady state, left ventricular output equals right ventricular output.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The Frank–Starling mechanism, an intrinsic property of cardiac muscle, matches left and right ventricular outputs beat by beat without neural input.",
wrong={0:"Atrial natriuretic peptide promotes natriuresis over hours; it does not adjust stroke volume beat by beat.",
1:"The Bainbridge reflex raises heart rate in response to atrial stretch, and it is neural, so it cannot operate in a denervated heart.",
2:"The baroreceptor reflex works through autonomic nerves, which a transplanted heart lacks.",
4:"Sympathetic stimulation needs intact cardiac nerves (or circulating catecholamines over a slower time scale); the matching here persists without innervation."},
eli5="When more blood comes back to the heart, the muscle gets stretched and automatically pushes harder. It's built into the muscle, so it works even in a transplanted heart with no nerves."),

